# MC-X-F2. L §6.4: a scenario row "replaces it on its span whatever the two rows' known dates, since a stipulation
# isn't news: a base row known on the scenario's own known day is replaced too (§4.6 step 2)"; this is §6.4's own
# example. The base enters beta_job (lives 2027-08-01.., known 2027-01-01); the scenario leave (from 2028-03-01)
# replaces the entry, known 2027-01-01 too, ending it 2028-02-29: one job on 2028-06-01 in the base, none in leave.
# snap10 refused the scenario (LOAD-REPLACE replace jobs + [beta_job]: no earlier row to replace).
=== m.tally
model m
  tally 2
  on 2027-01-01
  start 2027-01-01
  end 2028-12-31
source s "s"
kind jobs
version v1
  cite s
  known 2027-01-01
  jobs + [beta_job] lives 2027-08-01..
ask n: count(j in jobs on 2028-06-01)
=== s.tally
scenario leave over m
  tally 2
  from 2028-03-01
version v2
  cite s
  known 2027-01-01
  replace jobs + [beta_job] lives 2027-08-01..2028-02-29
ask n2: count(j in jobs on 2028-06-01) in leave
=== expect
n: 1
  cut 2027-01-01
n2: 0
  world: leave
  cut 2027-01-01
